The synchronization proof is straightforward: each index below `valid` has exactly one producer, and that producer writes its shared-memory entry before the unconditional barrier. All 96 threads execute the same barrier, which makes those writes visible to thread zero before it reads precisely entries 0 through `valid - 1`. No other shared entries are read, and no input index outside that range is loaded. For `valid == 0`, all threads still reach the barrier, thread zero performs no reads and writes zero; for `valid == 96`, all entries are initialized and summed. The 64-bit accumulator can represent the sum of any 96 signed 32-bit integers, and the completion event is recorded after the kernel in the same stream, so successful event synchronization establishes completion before the host reads the output.

Threads 70–95 skip the entire conditional in the original kernel: they do not load shared memory, call `__syncthreads()`, or write the output. Threads 0–69 reach a block barrier under a nonuniform condition, violating the block-wide synchronization contract; this is invalid but does not imply that every device must hang. Successful immediate launch checks establish submission success, not correct barrier participation or successful asynchronous execution.

