The precise unboundedness test is a strict improvement `dist[u] + w < dist[v]` after K−1 rounds, with `dist[u]` finite and both endpoints able to reach T. Without a reachable negative cycle, any walk can have its nonnegative cycles removed without increasing cost, leaving a simple path with at most K−1 edges; therefore an improvement in round K is impossible. Conversely, a reachable negative cycle prevents all its edge inequalities from being satisfied: summing those inequalities around the cycle would imply its total weight is nonnegative. Thus at least one edge improves, and the cycle can be repeated before continuing to T to obtain arbitrarily negative costs.

An unchanged round is a fixed point, so early exit is safe. When S equals T, the empty walk gives cost zero unless a negative cycle reachable from S can return to S; negative self-loops are detected even when K equals one. Parallel edges need no special handling.

