Invariant: after round r, `dist[v]` is the minimum cost of an S-to-v walk using at most r edges in the retained graph, or `None` if no such walk exists. Copying distances preserves shorter walks, and computing candidates only from the previous round extends them by exactly one edge. The `dist[u] is None` guard prevents unreachable distances from participating in arithmetic.

Every S-to-T walk lies entirely among vertices that can reach T, so filtering loses no candidate walk. If there is no reachable negative cycle in that graph, removing nonnegative cycles gives a minimum-cost simple path with at most K−1 edges. Therefore a strict improvement on the extra, Kth round—specifically `dist[u] + w < dist[v]` from a reachable u—certifies a reachable negative cycle that can reach T. Conversely, such a cycle prevents distances from reaching a fixed point: its edge inequalities cannot all hold, because summing them would assert that its negative total weight is nonnegative. Repeating that cycle before continuing to T gives arbitrarily negative costs.

An unchanged round is a fixed point, so its early return is safe. When S equals T, the empty walk gives cost zero unless a reachable negative cycle can return to S; a negative self-loop is detected even when K equals one.

Worst-case time is O(N + M + K(N + M)), hence O(N(N + M)), because each synchronous round copies N distances and scans at most M edges. Memory is O(N + M). These deterministic bounds hold for every allowed input. Candidates use at most K edges, so their absolute costs are at most K·10^12 ≤ 2·10^15; Python integers represent them exactly.

