The initialization proof follows the exact indices: thread zero reads only `entries[i]` for `0 <= i < valid`. For each such index, thread `i` exists because `valid <= 96`, satisfies the load condition, and initializes `entries[i]` before reaching the barrier. All 96 threads reach that same barrier, which makes those writes visible before thread zero reads them. Entries from `valid` through 95 may remain uninitialized, but are never read; input reads are likewise restricted to indices below `valid`.

When `valid == 0`, all threads skip the loads but still execute the barrier. Thread zero initializes its local sum to zero, executes no loop iterations, and writes zero to `*output`. The host neither skips the kernel nor initializes the device output. Accumulating at most 96 CUDA 32-bit integers into a `long long` also avoids integer overflow. The event synchronization checks execution completion before the host reads the result, though completion alone is not a general proof of numerical or memory correctness.

B’s wait references E’s first recording, the most recent recording when that wait was submitted; re-recording E does not retarget an existing wait. P2 can race with C1 because neither stream orders P2 after C1. I’m interpreting “weight” as “wait” and “save first use” as “safe first use.”

A permitted trace is:
1. The host submits P1, records E, submits B’s wait and C1, then submits P2 and records E again.
2. P1 writes 1 and the first recording completes, satisfying B’s wait.
3. P2 starts overwriting the allocation while C1 can read it. Querying E now returns not ready because the query concerns the second recording, which has not completed—not the first recording captured by B.
4. P2 can finish writing 2 before C1 performs its read, so C1 can read 2 rather than P1’s intended 1. Overlapping accesses are also possible; no ownership guarantee exists.

The corrected loop orders each slot’s operations as producer → ready → consumer → consumed → next producer. First use skips the consumed wait because no previous consumer exists. All waits inside the loop are device-side stream waits; the only host completion wait is after all iterations.

```cpp
// ownership.cu
#include <cuda_runtime.h>
#include <cstdio>
#include <cstdlib>

#define CHECK(call) do {                                         \
    cudaError_t status = (call);                                 \
    if (status != cudaSuccess) {                                 \
        std::fprintf(stderr, "%s: %s\n", #call,                  \
                     cudaGetErrorString(status));                \
        std::exit(EXIT_FAILURE);                                \
    }                                                           \
} while (0)

__global__ void produce(int* slot, int value) {
    *slot = value;
}

__global__ void consume(const int* slot, int* result) {
    *result = *slot;
}

struct Slot {
    int* data = nullptr;
    cudaEvent_t ready;
    cudaEvent_t consumed;
    bool used = false;
};

int main() {
    constexpr int iterations = 8;
    cudaStream_t A, B;
    CHECK(cudaStreamCreateWithFlags(&A, cudaStreamNonBlocking));
    CHECK(cudaStreamCreateWithFlags(&B, cudaStreamNonBlocking));

    Slot slots[2];
    for (Slot& slot : slots) {
        CHECK(cudaMalloc(reinterpret_cast<void**>(&slot.data),
                         sizeof(int)));
        CHECK(cudaEventCreateWithFlags(&slot.ready,
                                       cudaEventDisableTiming));
        CHECK(cudaEventCreateWithFlags(&slot.consumed,
                                       cudaEventDisableTiming));
    }

    int* results = nullptr;
    CHECK(cudaMalloc(reinterpret_cast<void**>(&results),
                     iterations * sizeof(int)));

    for (int i = 0; i < iterations; ++i) {
        Slot& slot = slots[i % 2];

        // Capture the previous consumed recording before reuse.
        // First use has no previous consumer to wait for.
        if (slot.used) {
            CHECK(cudaStreamWaitEvent(A, slot.consumed, 0));
        }

        produce<<<1, 1, 0, A>>>(slot.data, i + 1);
        CHECK(cudaGetLastError());
        CHECK(cudaEventRecord(slot.ready, A));

        // Capture this iteration's ready recording now.
        CHECK(cudaStreamWaitEvent(B, slot.ready, 0));
        consume<<<1, 1, 0, B>>>(slot.data, results + i);
        CHECK(cudaGetLastError());
        CHECK(cudaEventRecord(slot.consumed, B));
        slot.used = true;
    }

    // Final completion only: no host waits between iterations.
    CHECK(cudaStreamSynchronize(B));

    int host_results[iterations];
    CHECK(cudaMemcpy(host_results, results, sizeof(host_results),
                     cudaMemcpyDeviceToHost));
    for (int i = 0; i < iterations; ++i) {
        if (host_results[i] != i + 1) {
            std::fprintf(stderr, "iteration %d: got %d, expected %d\n",
                         i, host_results[i], i + 1);
            std::exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
        }
    }

    for (Slot& slot : slots) {
        CHECK(cudaEventDestroy(slot.ready));
        CHECK(cudaEventDestroy(slot.consumed));
        CHECK(cudaFree(slot.data));
    }
    CHECK(cudaFree(results));
    CHECK(cudaStreamDestroy(A));
    CHECK(cudaStreamDestroy(B));
    std::puts("All results passed.");
    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
```

Compile and run with `nvcc -std=c++17 ownership.cu -o ownership && ./ownership` on a CUDA-capable system.

For iteration i, B captures ready[i] before that slot’s ready event is re-recorded. On reuse at i+2, A captures consumed[i] before its next recording, so the old consumer finishes before the slot is overwritten. These dependencies always point to previously submitted work, so they introduce no dependency cycle. Each result has its own allocation element, and final synchronization of B establishes completion of every consumer and, through their ready waits, every producer before cleanup. Immediate launch checks alone do not establish execution success; the final checked stream synchronization also checks asynchronous completion.

```cpp
#include <cuda_runtime.h>

__device__ float broadcast_lane18(float value)
{
    const unsigned linearThread =
        threadIdx.x + blockDim.x *
        (threadIdx.y + blockDim.y * threadIdx.z);
    const unsigned lane = linearThread % 32u;
    constexpr unsigned mask = 0x0007ffffu;

    for (unsigned stride = 1u; stride <= 16u; stride <<= 1u) {
        const bool hasPartner =
            (lane % (2u * stride) == 0u) &&
            (lane + stride <= 18u);
        const unsigned source = hasPartner ? lane + stride : lane;

        const float partner =
            __shfl_sync(mask, value, static_cast<int>(source), 32);

        if (hasPartner) {
            value = __fadd_rn(value, partner);
        }
    }

    return __shfl_sync(mask, value, 0, 32);
}
```
Yes. A caller with no partner still executes the stage’s `__shfl_sync`, choosing its own lane as `source`; it skips the addition because `hasPartner` is false. Every caller also executes the final broadcast collective.

A lane with no partner selects itself as the shuffle source, but does not add the returned value. In the original code:
```cpp
const unsigned source = hasPartner ? lane + stride : lane;

const float partner =
    __shfl_sync(mask, value, static_cast<int>(source), 32);

if (hasPartner) {
    value = __fadd_rn(value, partner);
}
```
When `hasPartner` is false, `source == lane`. Every caller is a physical lane from 0 through 18, and `mask = 0x0007ffffu` includes exactly those lanes. Each executes the shuffle unconditionally at every stride with the same mask and width, so the self-source is a participating caller without relying on implicit lockstep.

The shuffle returns that lane’s current `value` into `partner`, but the false guard skips the addition, leaving `value` unchanged. Thus its accumulated inputs are not counted a second time. For example, lane 18 at stride 1 has no partner because 19 exceeds 18: it reads lane 18, then skips the addition. At stride 2, lane 16 can read lane 18 and incorporate its input once into a distinct partial sum.

Finally, every caller executes `return __shfl_sync(mask, value, 0, 32);`. That copies lane 0’s completed reduction; it does not add the caller’s retained value to it.
